[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say otherwise. The desk
merged them by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and a
subject line like "Wrong day on the invite" fits three different bugs.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued fix is for the parser. It does not fix the form hint or the one-day move. Those reports
stay open after #4471 closes.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## The three problems

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. A tenant whose locale writes the day
  first gets the day and month swapped: 7 April becomes 4 July. Any channel.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the date the
  tenant's way, day first. People type what the hint tells them and the date swaps. Only the form
  channel, only booking-v3 tenants. The parser fix does not touch it.
- **`one-day move`**: the date moves by one day, not by month. Monday instead of Tuesday, an invite
  before its booking, Lisbon and Toronto on different days, "only June". That reads as a time zone
  or summer-time bug. The desk had these flagged in its notes and still counted them as `parser`.

## How the reports are placed

By the tenant, not by the subject and not by intake's tag. Intake tags `hint` when the reporter's
browser is `en-US`; that is a guess about the person, not the form. The tenant directory says which
locale and which booking form each tenant runs, and `src/lib/kb/problems.ts` places each report from
that and the desk's notes. The table above runs it on every build, so new reports land in the right
row without anyone editing this page.

## Lessons

- A shared subject is not a shared cause. Merge on what broke, not on what people wrote.
- A note that says "these are different" belongs in the count, not in a footnote under it.

